---
/**
 * Global Scientific Canvas — the page-level background system (v1.6).
 *
 * v1.4 and v1.5 both treated the scientific field as *section decoration*: a
 * layer dropped inside a `<section>`, at 2.5–5.5% opacity, cropped to that
 * section's box. The result was a background nobody could see. v1.6 makes it
 * what the owner asked for: one canvas behind the entire document, mathematics
 * / biology / AI as the environment every section sits in.
 *
 * Structure — two layers, both inline SVG, both inert:
 *
 *   Layer A  MacroScienceLayer   280–800px scientific compositions
 *   Layer B  MicroNotebookLayer  notation, ticks, matrices, links
 *
 * Placement contract:
 *
 *   body (position: relative)
 *     └── <GlobalScientificCanvas />   ← this file, z-index: -1
 *     ├── <Header />
 *     ├── <main>…</main>
 *     └── <Footer />
 *
 * It is `position: absolute` — document-level, not viewport-fixed — so it
 * scrolls with the page and its plaques stay attached to the sections they
 * sit behind. A fixed layer would read as wallpaper; this reads as a very
 * large sheet of research paper.
 *
 * Accessibility and performance:
 *
 *   · aria-hidden="true" — never read, never announced;
 *   · pointer-events: none — never in the way of a link or a button;
 *   · inline SVG only — no <canvas>, no WebGL, no three.js, no image, no
 *     fetch, no extra request of any kind;
 *   · every stroke and fill resolves to a CSS custom property, so one SVG
 *     serves paper, white and night;
 *   · the only motion is two very slow drifts, disabled under
 *     prefers-reduced-motion.
 */
import MacroScienceLayer from './MacroScienceLayer.astro';
import MicroNotebookLayer from './MicroNotebookLayer.astro';
---

<div class="global-science" data-global-scientific-canvas aria-hidden="true">
  <MacroScienceLayer />
  <MicroNotebookLayer />
</div>
